SkipListSet Java Implementation

Overview

This project is an implementation of a genericized SkipListSet<T> in Java, which implements the SortedSet<T> interface. The skip list is a probabilistic data structure that provides an efficient way to maintain a sorted collection while offering expected logarithmic time complexity for search, insert, and delete operations.

The implementation includes three main components:

SkipListSet: The primary class implementing SortedSet<T>, which manages the skip list nodes and handles all operations like add, remove, and search.

SkipListSetIterator: An internal iterator class that provides the functionality to iterate through the skip list in sorted order.

SkipListNode: An internal node wrapper class representing individual elements in the skip list.

Features

Implements SortedSet<T> and hence supports all the methods defined in Set<T>, Collection<T>, and Iterable<T>.

Logarithmic time complexity for add, remove, and search operations.

Supports bulk operations such as addAll() and removeAll().

Includes a rebalancing mechanism (reBalance()) to optimize the height of the skip list nodes.

Class Structure

SkipListSet

Implements SortedSet<T> with methods like add(), remove(), contains(), first(), last(), etc.

Uses an internal linked list structure to maintain multiple levels of nodes, enabling faster traversal.

Balances itself after bulk operations to maintain optimal performance.

SkipListSetIterator

Provides an implementation of Iterator<T> to traverse through the skip list.

Supports the hasNext(), next(), and remove() methods for iteration.

SkipListNode

Represents the nodes in the skip list.

Contains a value and an ArrayList of pointers (nextNodes) for maintaining references at various levels.

Requirements

Java 8 or later.

The skip list must be compiled with javac SkipListSet.java.

Testing requires the SkipListTestHarness.java to validate functionality and performance.

Installation and Compilation

Clone the repository or download the source files.

Compile the SkipListSet and SkipListTestHarness files:

javac SkipListSet.java SkipListTestHarness.java

Run the test harness to evaluate the skip list's performance and correctness:

java SkipListTestHarness

Testing

The project includes SkipListTestHarness.java, which runs a series of test cases to validate the correctness and performance of the skip list implementation. The tests compare the SkipListSet with other data structures like TreeSet and LinkedList for a variety of operations including addition, search, deletion, and balancing.

Sample Test Output

CASE: 100,000 strings of length 1,000, 10,000 finds, 5,000 removals. Generating...
  SkipListSet add:    241ms  find:     18ms  del:     75ms  (5,236 missing) find:     27ms
                                             bal:     50ms  (5,236 missing) find:     18ms
  TreeSet     add:     72ms  find:      8ms  del:      6ms  (5,236 missing) find:      9ms



  Performance

The skip list implementation exhibits logarithmic performance characteristics for add, remove, and search operations.

Balancing and managing levels are done probabilistically to ensure  complexity.

The performance is comparable to TreeSet in most cases and is validated by running against large test datasets.


